            "sampleAnswer": "The sea offers an extraordinarily diverse range of activities that appeal to people with very different interests and physical capabilities, which is part of what makes coastal environments so universally popular as holiday destinations. At the most accessible end, simply being near the sea—walking along the shoreline, sitting on the beach, watching the waves—has a well-documented restorative effect on wellbeing that requires no equipment or particular fitness. Swimming is probably the most widely practised active pursuit, ranging from gentle paddling in calm waters to serious open-water swimming. Water sports—surfing, kayaking, paddleboarding, sailing, windsurfing, and kitesurfing—offer more demanding physical challenges and have grown significantly in popularity. Snorkelling and scuba diving open up the underwater world to those interested in marine life and ecosystems. Fishing—both recreational and competitive—has a large and dedicated following in most coastal communities. I think what's particularly interesting about coastal activities is the way they span such a wide range of engagement—from the completely passive to the intensely athletic—and the way the sea itself, with its variability and unpredictability, makes even simple activities feel more alive and more present than their inland equivalents.",
