            "sampleAnswer": "No, I don't think talent alone is sufficient to explain fame, and I think the relationship between talent and fame is more complex and more contingent than we often assume. Talent is certainly a necessary condition for sustained fame in most fields—people who achieve lasting recognition in music, sport, literature, or science generally do have genuine ability—but it's far from sufficient. The history of any creative or competitive field is full of people of extraordinary talent who never achieved significant public recognition, and of people of more modest talent who achieved enormous fame. I think the factors that determine whether talent translates into fame include luck—being in the right place at the right time, having the right connections, or producing work that happens to resonate with the cultural moment—as well as personal qualities that are distinct from talent itself, like charisma, persistence, self-promotion, and the ability to navigate the social and institutional structures of a given field. The rise of social media has added another dimension—the ability to build and maintain a public persona, to engage with audiences, and to generate attention has become increasingly important, and these are skills that are distinct from the underlying talent that might originally attract attention. I also think there's a significant role for institutional support—the backing of a label, a publisher, a club, or an agent can be decisive in determining whether talent reaches a wide audience.",
